1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,200
Fire does not behave in microgravity
the way it does on Earth.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,800
Without normal buoyancy, flames can spread,
extinguish, and produce smoke differently.

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,800
NASA has spent decades studying these
through experiments like BASS-II and FLEX,

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,200
as well as SPICE, SAFFIRE,
and the SAME investigation.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,800
We are Team FLARE-X. I'm Saber Hossain
Mahim, Product and Science Lead,

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,200
joined by Ismail Hossen, Data Lead,
and Mahzabin Muntaha, ML Lead,

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,000
with Abdullah Al Masum, Systems Lead,
Nahid, Frontend Lead,

8
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:36,500
and Soyebuzaman Naim,
Media and Submission Lead.

9
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:41,800
Our project is FLARE-X:
NASA Microgravity Fire Intelligence,

10
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:46,800
designed to turn scattered NASA data
into evidence-backed fire intelligence.

11
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,200
The problem is not that NASA lacks data.

12
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,000
The problem is knowing which experiment
actually applies to your question.

13
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,000
Solid materials, liquid droplets,
gaseous flames, spacecraft-scale fires,

14
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:06,000
and smoke studies use different fuels,
geometries, oxygen levels, and pressures,

15
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,200
as well as varied airflow conditions
and diagnostic measurements.

16
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,800
Their results may live in tables, reports,
publications, images, or flight video.

17
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,200
A normal document search
can find the right words.

18
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:24,200
But keyword similarity does not tell you
if two tests are physically comparable,

19
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,800
whether their outcomes mean the same thing,
or whether a model is appropriate,

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,500
or whether a new scenario lies beyond
the conditions NASA actually tested.

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,200
For fire safety, the real task is to find,
compare, and interpret experiments safely.

22
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,500
FLARE-X is designed to bridge that gap.

23
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,200
A user describes a scenario in natural
language or structured parameters.

24
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:52,000
The system converts it to a scenario,
identifies the combustion family,

25
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,800
retrieves relevant NASA experiments,
and ranks them by physical similarity.

26
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:01,800
FLARE-X then routes supported questions
to a compatible quantitative model.

27
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,800
SPICE can support flame-length
regression for gaseous flames.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,500
FLEX can support extinction
classification for droplet combustion.

29
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:16,500
BASS-II can support solid-fire analysis
where the extracted labels are strong.

30
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,500
But FLARE-X does not trust a model
simply because it returns a number.

31
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,500
Before presenting a prediction, an
Envelope Guard checks experimental support

32
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:31,500
across material, oxygen, pressure,
airflow, geometry, and local neighborhood.

33
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,500
FLARE-X knows when not to predict.

34
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,500
That becomes visible in the
Experimental Support Map.

35
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,200
NASA experiments remain visible while
the user’s scenario moves through space.

36
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,000
Now change oxygen while holding
the other selected variables constant.

37
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:54,800
FLARE-X does not just recalculate.
It reranks evidence and rechecks support.

38
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:00,000
As the marker moves, both predicted
regime and evidence domain can change:

39
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:04,500
inside, near the boundary,
partially supported,

40
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:09,200
and finally outside the
NASA-supported model domain.

41
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:14,200
At that point, FLARE-X stops
treating the prediction as reliable.

42
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:19,200
The prediction is withheld, while the
nearest NASA experiments remain visible.

43
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:24,200
Every conclusion carries provenance.

44
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:29,500
Direct NASA observations, model inference,
and AI synthesis remain distinct.

45
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,800
FLARE-X is a decision-support concept,
not operational safety software.

46
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
During the hackathon, we plan to validate
this workflow using NASA combustion data.

47
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The goal is simple: find the evidence,
understand its limits,

48
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:48,800
and make NASA's microgravity fire
knowledge easier to reuse.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,800
FLARE-X. Evidence-bounded AI
for microgravity fire science.
